/-!
# Factorizing an almost isomorphism into glueings and squishings

Let `k` be an algebraically closed field and let `f : X' ⟶ X` be a finite morphism of algebraic
`k`-schemes such that `𝒪_X ⊂ f_*𝒪_{X'}` and such that `f` is an isomorphism away from a finite
set of points. [Stacks, Tag 0C1L] factors `f` as

`X' = X_n ⟶ X_{n-1} ⟶ ⋯ ⟶ X_1 ⟶ X_0 = X`

in which every step is either the glueing of two rational points ([Stacks, Tag 0C1J]) or the
squishing of a tangent vector ([Stacks, Tag 0C1K]).

This file does **not** prove that lemma. It proves, unconditionally, the two halves of the
argument that can be isolated from the geometry: the *local algebra of a minimal step*, which is
the mathematical heart of the proof, and the *induction on the length*, which is its skeleton.
The one statement that is still missing is written out as the hypothesis `hsplit` of
`Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.isElementaryChain_of_isAlmostIso`, so the remaining gap is a Lean
statement rather than a remark.

## Main definitions

* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.IsElementaryStep` says that a morphism of `k`-schemes is one of the
  two elementary modifications of [Stacks, Tag 0C1L]: a glueing of two rational points or a
  squishing of a tangent vector.
* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.IsElementaryChain` says that a morphism is a composite of finitely
  many elementary modifications and isomorphisms. This is the conclusion of
  [Stacks, Tag 0C1L].
* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.IsAlmostIso` collects the hypotheses of [Stacks, Tag 0C1L] on a
  morphism: both schemes are algebraic over `k`, the morphism is finite, its structure-sheaf map
  is injective, and it restricts to an isomorphism over the complement of a finite set. All of
  these but the injectivity are `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.IsAlmostIsoCore`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.algEquiv_prod_or_dualNumber_of_minimal` — **the local algebra of a
  minimal step**, unconditional. Let `A` be a local `k`-algebra whose residue field is `k` and
  let `B` be a module-finite `A`-algebra which is *not* generated by `A` and which has no
  `A`-subalgebra strictly between `A` and `B`. Then the fibre `B ⧸ m_A B` is, as a `k`-algebra,
  either `k × k` or the dual numbers `k[ε]`. In the proof of [Stacks, Tag 0C1L] this is the step
  "`C` has exactly two `k`-subalgebras, thus `C = k × k` or `C = k[ε]`", and it is exactly what
  distinguishes a glueing from a squishing.
* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_algebraMap_eq_of_mem_map_maximalIdeal` — **`m_A B ⊆ A` for a
  minimal extension**, unconditional. Under the same hypotheses the ideal `m_A B` lies inside the
  image of `A`. This is the source's trichotomy on `A ⊂ A + m_A B ⊂ B` carried out correctly; see
  its docstring for the correction to the source that it records.
* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.prod_fst_eq_snd_of_algebraMap`,
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_algebraMap_eq_of_prod_fst_eq_snd` and
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_prod_fst_sub_snd_eq` — **the fibre sequence of a glueing**: when
  the fibre is `k × k`, the image of `A` in `B` is exactly the equalizer of the two projections,
  and their difference is surjective onto `k`. These three are the algebraic content of the three
  germ-level clauses of `AlgebraicGeometry.IsGlueingTwoPoints`, read at the stalk.
* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.dualNumber_snd_eq_zero_of_algebraMap`,
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_algebraMap_eq_of_dualNumber_snd_eq_zero` and
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_dualNumber_snd_eq` — **the fibre sequence of a squishing**: the
  same three statements for a fibre `k[ε]`, with the `ε`-coefficient in place of the difference
  of the two projections.
* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.isElementaryChain_of_split` — **the induction on the length**,
  unconditional. For any notion of admissible morphism and any `ℕ`-valued measure on
  morphisms, if every admissible morphism is either already a chain of elementary modifications
  or splits into two admissible morphisms of strictly smaller measure, then every admissible
  morphism is a chain.
* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.isElementaryChain_of_isAlmostIso` — the previous result specialised
  to the hypotheses of [Stacks, Tag 0C1L], conditional on its hypothesis `hsplit`. That
  hypothesis is discharged in
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.SquishingProducer`, where the
  unconditional lemma `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.IsAlmostIso.isElementaryChain` carries the tag.
* `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.IsElementaryStep.isFinite` and
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.IsElementaryChain.isFinite` — both notions imply finiteness, which is
  the sanity check that the packaging of the conclusion is not weaker than the source's.

## Implementation notes

**The packaging of the factorization.** The source writes the conclusion as an explicit chain
`X' = X_n ⟶ ⋯ ⟶ X_0 = X`. An explicit chain forces a choice of indexing — `ComposableArrows`,
a `Fin (n + 1)`-indexed family, a `List` — and then every use of it has to re-derive the one
fact a chain is for, namely that the two ends compose to `f`. Here the conclusion is instead the
inductive predicate `IsElementaryChain`, generated by the isomorphisms, the elementary steps and
composition. It says precisely "`f` is a composite of finitely many elementary modifications",
with the endpoint identifications of the source absorbed into the `of_isIso` constructor (which
is also what makes the degenerate case `n = 0` of the source — "`f` is an isomorphism and there
is nothing to prove" — a case of the predicate rather than an exception to it). Its recursor is
the descending induction the source's proof performs, so a consumer gets the induction for free
instead of reconstructing it from an index.

Spelled out, `IsElementaryChain k f` says: `f` is a composite `X' = Y_n ⟶ ⋯ ⟶ Y_0 = X` in which
every arrow is an elementary modification *or an isomorphism*. The source's chain has no
isomorphisms interspersed, so the source's conclusion is formally the stronger of the two; they
agree once one knows that an elementary modification composed with an isomorphism is again one,
which is true but is not proved here (it means transporting all of the sheaf clauses of
`AlgebraicGeometry.IsGlueingTwoPoints` along an isomorphism). What is delivered is therefore the
conclusion of [Stacks, Tag 0C1L] read up to isomorphism of the intermediate schemes, which is
the reading every consumer of the lemma in the source uses.

The chain lives in `CategoryTheory.Over (Spec (.of k))` rather than on bare schemes carrying
`Scheme.Over` instances: an inductive family cannot be indexed by instance arguments, and
`CategoryTheory.OverClass.fromOver` recovers `Y.left.Over (Spec (.of k))` from a bundled object
`Y`, so the two conventions interconvert with no local instance declaration. Finite type and
quasi-compactness of the structure morphisms are carried as conjuncts inside `IsAlmostIso`,
matching `AlgebraicGeometry.IsGlueingTwoPoints` and
`AlgebraicGeometry.IsSquishingTangentVector` rather than an instance-binder
convention.

**The splitting hypothesis.** `isElementaryChain_of_isAlmostIso` reduces [Stacks, Tag 0C1L] to
one statement, its hypothesis `hsplit`: *there is a measure `μ` such that every `f` satisfying
`IsAlmostIso` either is a chain of elementary modifications, or factors as `p ≫ q` with `p` and
`q` again satisfying `IsAlmostIso` and both of strictly smaller measure.* The source produces
this from two ingredients, and the state of each is as follows.

* *The factorization attached to a subalgebra is available.* The relative spectrum of a
  quasi-coherent sheaf of algebras is in the library
  (`Stacks.AffineAlgebraEquivalence.relativeSpec`), and with it the dictionary between
  intermediate factorizations and subalgebras, in
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.IntermediateFactorization`. Given a
  finite quasi-coherent `𝒜` with `𝒪_X ⊂ 𝒜 ⊂ f_*𝒪_{X'}`,
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_split_isAlmostIso_of_algebraHom` produces the factorization
  `X' ⟶ Spec_X(𝒜) ⟶ X` and proves `IsAlmostIso` of *both* halves. The one clause of
  `IsAlmostIso` which is not a restriction of the given data, the inclusion
  `𝒪_{Spec_X(𝒜)} ⊂ p_*𝒪_{X'}` for the upper half `p : X' ⟶ Spec_X(𝒜)`, is `𝒜 ⊂ f_*𝒪_{X'}`
  read on the charts of `Spec_X(𝒜)`, which are the basic opens of `𝒜(V)` rather than the
  preimages of opens of `X`; that is the exactness of localisation, and it is proved in
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.PushforwardMono`. The subalgebra `𝒜`
  itself is produced in
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.SubalgebraProducer`, from a single
  section of `f_*𝒪_{X'}` over an affine open, so that
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_split_isAlmostIso_of_section` takes no subalgebra as a
  hypothesis and records that the splitting captures the section.
* *The measure is missing.* It is the length of the coherent `𝒪_X`-module `f_*𝒪_{X'}/𝒪_X`,
  which the source shows is a finite direct sum of skyscrapers of finite length. Its stalkwise
  ingredient is in the library: `Stacks.Chow.sheafMultiplicity` is the length of the stalk of a
  sheaf of modules over the local ring. The *finiteness* of those lengths is now proved, at
  every point, by
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.isFiniteLength_stalk_cokernel_structureUnitHom` in
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.BadPointsLength`, as is finiteness of
  the support of the quotient. The additivity of length along
  `0 → 𝒜/𝒪_X → f_*𝒪_{X'}/𝒪_X → f_*𝒪_{X'}/𝒜 → 0`, the fibre sum comparing the measure of the
  upper half with lengths over `𝒪_{X, x}`, strictness at both ends, and the measure itself are
  all proved — `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.measureOver` in
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.BadPointsSum`. What is still missing is the identification of each
  half's measure with the corresponding drop; the implementation notes of
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.SubalgebraProducer` and of
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.StalkSubalgebraSpreadOut` set those
  out. Nothing *here* constructs a `μ`.
* *The one-step classification is missing on the geometric side.* If no proper intermediate
  factorization exists then `f` should be a glueing or a squishing. Its *algebraic* content is
  `algEquiv_prod_or_dualNumber_of_minimal`, proved here: the intermediate factorizations
  `X' ⟶ Y ⟶ X` with `𝒪_X ⊂ g_*𝒪_Y ⊂ f_*𝒪_{X'}` correspond to `𝒪_X`-subalgebras of
  `f_*𝒪_{X'}`, so "no proper intermediate factorization" is the hypothesis `hmin` below, and
  the conclusion `k × k` versus `k[ε]` is glueing versus squishing. `hmin` is not a hypothesis
  the splitting dichotomy hands over for free — the negation of its branch is only
  `m_x·(f_*𝒪_{X'})_x ⊆ 𝒪_{X,x}` — and it is now derived from the absence of a proper intermediate
  quasi-coherent subalgebra by
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.forall_eq_bot_or_eq_top_of_forall_isFinite_subalg` in
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.StalkSubalgebraSpreadOut`. The
  germ-level sheaf
  sequences of `AlgebraicGeometry.IsGlueingTwoPoints` and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.IsSquishingTangentVector` are proved here *at the stalk* — the six results
  listed above. On the *glueing* side they are now carried to the germs of sections over the
  opens `f ⁻¹ᵁ U`, which is what that predicate quantifies over: see
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.sectionAt_pointOfStalkHom` and, for the whole predicate,
  `Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_isGlueingTwoPoints_of_fibre_algEquiv_prod`, which proves
  `AlgebraicGeometry.IsGlueingTwoPoints` in full from the hypotheses of the minimal step together
  with one extra hypothesis and nothing else. That extra hypothesis is the clause
  `IsIso (f ∣_ U)` for `U` the complement of the single bad point, which is a statement about the
  whole bad set rather than about one point and is what still separates the glueing half of the
  one-step classification from the lemma. On the *squishing* side the predicate does not use
  germ-local clauses for its exactness at all, so there is nothing of the same shape to transport;
  see the implementation notes of
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.MinimalStep`. What the passage to the
  local algebra does have is the stalk: for a finite `f` the stalk `(f_*𝒪_{X'})_x` is the
  localisation of the sections over the preimage of an affine open
  (`AlgebraicGeometry.IsAffineOpen.isLocalization_pushforwardStalk`) and is module-finite over
  `𝒪_{X, x}` (`Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.module_finite_stalk_pushforward`), which is the
  hypothesis `Module.Finite A B` below. It is *not* the product of the local rings over the
  fibre — see the implementation notes of
  `StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Curves.GlueingSquishing.FiniteStalk` for why, and for which
  ring is the product of local rings.

**The local algebra.** Three things happen in `algEquiv_prod_or_dualNumber_of_minimal`. The
hypothesis that the residue field of `A` is `k` is spelled as `hres`, "every element of `A` is a
scalar modulo `m_A`", which is the form in which it is used and is equivalent to surjectivity of
`k → A → A/m_A`. It makes the image of `A` in the fibre `B ⧸ m_A B` equal to the image of `k`,
which is what lets a `k`-subalgebra of the fibre be pulled back to an `A`-subalgebra of `B`
(`Subalgebra.mk` applied to the `k`-subalgebra `Subalgebra.comap (Ideal.Quotient.mkₐ k _) D`,
whose membership is definitionally the right thing, hence the `Iff.rfl`). That correspondence
transports `hmin` to the fibre. Nondegeneracy of the fibre is Nakayama in the form
`Submodule.le_of_le_smul_of_le_jacobson_bot`, applied with `N' = ⊤` and `N` the image of `A`:
the hypothesis `⊤ ≤ N ⊔ m_A • ⊤` is exactly the source's `B = A + m_A B`, and `m_A • ⊤` is the
ideal `m_A B` by `Ideal.smul_top_eq_map`. With both in hand,
`Algebra.algEquiv_prod_or_dualNumber_of_no_intermediateSubalgebra` ([Stacks, Tag 0C1I]) finishes.
Note that `Module.Finite A B` is used only through Nakayama.

**The fibre sequence.** The six results about the fibre share one step, and it is the step that
needs the correction to the source recorded in
`Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_algebraMap_eq_of_mem_map_maximalIdeal`: an element of `B` which
the characters cannot distinguish from a scalar has a class in `B ⧸ m_A B` lying in the image of
`k`, hence differs from an element of `A` by an element of `m_A B` — and *that* is in `A` only
because `m_A B ⊆ A`. The subalgebra `A + m_A B` witnessing the latter is
`Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.addMapMaximalIdeal`, a subalgebra because `m_A B` is an ideal of `B`.
The two cases differ only in which function of the fibre is used as the character: the difference
of the two projections of `k × k`, or the `ε`-coefficient of `k[ε]`. Neither is packaged as a
ring or module map — the first is not a ring map at all, and both are used by the geometric side
through the ring maps `B ⟶ k` and `B ⟶ k[ε]` that
`Stacks.GlueSquishFactor.exists_glueing_or_squishing_data_of_minimal` already builds from the
same equivalence.

